<!--
  The Windows installer: installs usage-control.exe as the Windows service
  "Usage Control", which starts with Windows, and opens its port in the
  Windows firewall for private networks only. By default the service only
  serves this PC's usage to a hub (DATA_ONLY); with WEBSITE=1 it also shows
  the website and keeps the history itself. Double-clicking the installer
  asks which of the two this PC should do (the "Mode" page below); a newer
  version's installer asks again, and a repair of the installed version
  switches with the option (msiexec /i usage-control.msi REINSTALL=ALL
  REINSTALLMODE=m WEBSITE=1).

  Add-ons are optional programs that track more, each a service of its own
  that writes to C:\ProgramData\Usage Control\addons, which usage-control
  shows. The Add-ons page offers each one as a box, and each has an install
  option. POWER=1 installs the power add-on, usage-control-power.exe, as the
  Windows service "Usage Control power add-on", which reads the power the
  energy meters Windows offers report (CPU, memory and laptop meters), what
  the battery gives while the PC runs on it, and what NVIDIA GPUs draw.
  GPU=1 installs the gpu add-on, usage-control-gpu.exe, as the service
  "Usage Control gpu add-on", which reads the fan, clocks and more of NVIDIA
  GPUs.
  PRESSURE=1 installs the pressure add-on, usage-control-pressure.exe, as the
  service "Usage Control pressure add-on", which reads how many threads wait
  for a processor, how many pages are read from disk for memory and how busy
  the disks are.
  KERNEL=1 installs the kernel add-on, usage-control-kernel.exe, as "Usage
  Control kernel add-on", which reads context switches, interrupts, handles, TCP
  connections and retransmissions.
  WIFI=1 installs the Wi-Fi add-on, usage-control-wifi.exe, as "Usage
  Control Wi-Fi add-on", which reads the signal of each connected Wi-Fi
  adapter.
  MEMORY=1 installs the memory add-on, usage-control-memory.exe, as the
  service "Usage Control memory add-on", which reads the Memory performance
  counters.
  PORTS=1 installs the ports add-on, usage-control-ports.exe, as "Usage
  Control ports add-on", which lists the ports the PC listens on.
  SMART=1 installs the smart add-on, usage-control-smart.exe, as the
  service "Usage Control smart add-on", which reads the disks' health; it
  runs as LocalSystem, as only administrators may send disks SMART commands.
  PROCESSES=1 installs the processes add-on, usage-control-processes.exe, as
  "Usage Control processes add-on", which lists the processes using the most
  CPU and memory.

  It also installs usage-control-tray.exe, the icon in the taskbar that shows
  whether the service runs and pauses, stops or opens it. It starts when
  someone logs in, and at the end of the setup wizard, also after a repair
  from the wizard, which closes it. Logged-in users may start and stop the
  service, so its menu needs no administrator.

  Built by .github/workflows/windows.yml with WiX Toolset 5:
    wix build windows/usage-control.wxs -arch x64 -d Version=1.2.3 -d Bugfix=0 -d Binary=usage-control.exe
      -d TrayBinary=usage-control-tray.exe -d AddOns=. -d Icon=usage-control.ico
      -ext WixToolset.Firewall.wixext -ext WixToolset.Util.wixext -ext WixToolset.UI.wixext
      -o usage-control.msi

  Version is the installer's version; a bugfix release such as 1.2.3a puts
  its letters in the fourth number (1.2.3.1), and Bugfix is that number, 0
  for none.

  Install options (msiexec /i usage-control.msi PORT=8090 WEBSITE=1):
    PORT            port the PC is reachable on (default 9393)
    WEBSITE         1 to show the website and keep the history on this PC,
                    0 to only serve the usage to a hub (default 0)
    DEVICE_NAME     how this PC names itself: on its page with WEBSITE=1, and
                    as the name a hub offers when this PC is added from its
                    browser (default: the page says Host Hub, the hub offers
                    the PC's name)
    HUB_DEVICES     with WEBSITE=1, other devices to collect from (hub mode)
    HUB_ROUTERS     with WEBSITE=1, routers to show as devices, each as
                    name=protocol:address, such as Router=upnp:192.168.1.1 or
                    Box=fritzbox:monitor@192.168.178.1 (see docs/setup.md)
    ROUTER_PASSWORDS  the passwords of those routers that need a login, and
                    SNMP communities other than public, as name=password; not
                    remembered: the service encrypts them with DPAPI into
                    router-passwords.dpapi in its data folder at its next
                    start and deletes them from the registry. Routers it
                    already has a password for keep it without this
    RETENTION_DAYS  with WEBSITE=1, days of history to keep (default 30)
    HISTORY_MAX_ENTRIES  with WEBSITE=1, how many disks, sensors, network cards
                    and GPUs each the history keeps per device (default 64)
    BUFFER_HOURS    without WEBSITE=1, hours of usage kept for a hub that
                    cannot reach this PC, from 1 to 168 (default 24)
    ALLOWED_HOSTS   other names this PC answers to, besides its IP addresses,
                    localhost, its hostname and .local names; comma-separated
    DISK_PATHS      drives or folders whose disks are shown, comma-separated
                    (default: the system drive); the service does not start
                    while one cannot be read, and says which in the event log
    UPDATE_CHECK    with WEBSITE=1, false to stop the daily check for a newer
                    release (default true); the installer refuses a value
                    the service cannot read: it takes true, false, 1, 0, t
                    and f, in the cases the service takes, without spaces
                    around. To fix a bad remembered value of the installed
                    version, repair it with its own installer:
                      msiexec /i usage-control.msi REINSTALL=ALL REINSTALLMODE=m UPDATE_CHECK=true
    RESET_PASSWORD  with WEBSITE=1, true to delete the password for changing
                    devices when the service starts; only from an
                    administrator, and not remembered, so the next install,
                    update or repair without it turns it off. To use it with
                    the installed version, repair it with its own installer:
                      msiexec /i usage-control.msi REINSTALL=ALL REINSTALLMODE=m RESET_PASSWORD=true
                    and then once more the same way without RESET_PASSWORD
    POWER           1 to install the power add-on, 0 to remove it in an update or repair
                    (default: not installed)
    GPU             1 to install the gpu add-on, 0 to remove it in an update or repair
    PRESSURE        1 to install the pressure add-on, 0 to remove it in an update or repair
    KERNEL          1 to install the kernel add-on, 0 to remove it in an update or repair
    WIFI            1 to install the Wi-Fi add-on, 0 to remove it in an update or repair
    MEMORY          1 to install the memory add-on, 0 to remove it in an update or repair
    PORTS           1 to install the ports add-on, 0 to remove it in an update or repair
    SMART           1 to install the smart add-on, 0 to remove it in an update or repair
    PROCESSES       1 to install the processes add-on, 0 to remove it in an update or repair
                    (default: not installed); the installer refuses any
                    other value for these
  An update keeps the options of the installed version, except RESET_PASSWORD;
  options given to the update replace them. An option given empty counts as
  not given, so to clear a remembered PORT, DEVICE_NAME, HUB_DEVICES,
  HUB_ROUTERS, RETENTION_DAYS, HISTORY_MAX_ENTRIES, BUFFER_HOURS, ALLOWED_HOSTS,
  DISK_PATHS or UPDATE_CHECK, give it a single space, such as DISK_PATHS=" ",
  which means its default. WEBSITE and the add-ons take 0 to turn them off.
-->
<Wix xmlns="http://wixtoolset.org/schemas/v4/wxs"
     xmlns:fw="http://wixtoolset.org/schemas/v4/wxs/firewall"
     xmlns:ui="http://wixtoolset.org/schemas/v4/wxs/ui"
     xmlns:util="http://wixtoolset.org/schemas/v4/wxs/util">
  <Package Name="Usage Control"
           Manufacturer="usage-control"
           Version="$(Version)"
           UpgradeCode="C226A238-37CF-4C24-85FE-A29D04C94172"
           Scope="perMachine">
    <!-- Installing a newer version replaces the old one and keeps the history.
         A bugfix release only changes the fourth number of the version, which
         Windows Installer ignores, so the same version counts as an update too. -->
    <MajorUpgrade AllowSameVersionUpgrades="yes"
                  DowngradeErrorMessage="A newer version of Usage Control is already installed." />
    <!--
      For the same reason an older bugfix of the same version would replace a
      newer one, so each install remembers its bugfix number (Bugfix), and an
      installer of the same version (SAMEVERSION, which compares only the
      first three numbers) with a lower one refuses to install.
    -->
    <Upgrade Id="C226A238-37CF-4C24-85FE-A29D04C94172">
      <UpgradeVersion Minimum="$(Version)" Maximum="$(Version)" IncludeMinimum="yes" IncludeMaximum="yes"
                      OnlyDetect="yes" Property="SAMEVERSION" />
    </Upgrade>
    <Property Id="INSTALLEDBUGFIX">
      <RegistrySearch Id="RememberedBugfix" Root="HKLM" Key="SOFTWARE\Usage Control" Name="Bugfix" Type="raw" />
    </Property>
    <Launch Condition="Installed OR NOT SAMEVERSION OR NOT INSTALLEDBUGFIX OR INSTALLEDBUGFIX &lt;= $(Bugfix)"
            Message="A newer bugfix of this version of Usage Control is already installed." />
    <MediaTemplate EmbedCab="yes" />
    <!-- The mascot, in Apps and Features. -->
    <Icon Id="UsageControl.ico" SourceFile="$(Icon)" />
    <Property Id="ARPPRODUCTICON" Value="UsageControl.ico" />

    <!--
      Each option is remembered in the registry. AppSearch reads the remembered
      value, which would overwrite one given on the command line, so that one
      is saved before and put back after, in both the UI and the install.
    -->
    <?foreach Setting in PORT;WEBSITE;DEVICE_NAME;HUB_DEVICES;HUB_ROUTERS;RETENTION_DAYS;HISTORY_MAX_ENTRIES;BUFFER_HOURS;ALLOWED_HOSTS;DISK_PATHS;UPDATE_CHECK;POWER;GPU;PRESSURE;KERNEL;WIFI;MEMORY;PORTS;SMART;PROCESSES ?>
    <Property Id="$(Setting)" Secure="yes">
      <RegistrySearch Id="Remembered$(Setting)" Root="HKLM" Key="SOFTWARE\Usage Control" Name="$(Setting)" Type="raw" />
    </Property>
    <SetProperty Action="Save$(Setting)" Id="CMDLINE_$(Setting)" Value="[$(Setting)]" Before="AppSearch" Sequence="both" Condition="$(Setting)" />
    <SetProperty Action="Restore$(Setting)" Id="$(Setting)" Value="[CMDLINE_$(Setting)]" After="AppSearch" Sequence="both" Condition="CMDLINE_$(Setting)" />
    <?endforeach?>
    <!--
      RESET_PASSWORD is the one option that is not remembered: while it is set,
      every start of the service deletes the password, so it applies only to
      the install, update or repair it is given to. A standard user may start
      a repair of an installed program too, so the service gets it only from
      an administrator (ServiceResetPassword); MSIUSEREALADMINDETECTION makes
      AdminUser tell a real administrator, not only an elevated install.
    -->
    <Property Id="RESET_PASSWORD" Secure="yes" />
    <Property Id="MSIUSEREALADMINDETECTION" Value="1" />
    <SetProperty Action="ResetPasswordByAdmin" Id="ServiceResetPassword" Value="[RESET_PASSWORD]" After="DefaultWEBSITE" Sequence="execute" Condition="AdminUser AND RESET_PASSWORD" />

    <!--
      The service refuses to start with an UPDATE_CHECK it cannot read, and
      would then fail again and again, so the installer refuses it instead. It
      takes what the service takes, as Go's strconv.ParseBool reads it, except
      spaces around the value, which the service trims but a condition cannot;
      a single space means the default. The launch conditions run after the
      remembered options are read, so this also checks those, in a repair too,
      and names the option that fixes a bad remembered value. For the version
      that is installed, msiexec /i with only the option does nothing, so the
      command it names then repairs it with the option (UpdateCheckFix). Only
      an uninstall (msiexec /x, as from Apps and Features, which sets REMOVE)
      is not held up by it.
    -->
    <Property Id="UpdateCheckFix" Value="UPDATE_CHECK=true" />
    <SetProperty Action="UpdateCheckFixInstalled" Id="UpdateCheckFix" Value="REINSTALL=ALL REINSTALLMODE=m UPDATE_CHECK=true" Before="LaunchConditions" Sequence="both" Condition="Installed" />
    <Launch Condition='REMOVE = "ALL" OR NOT UPDATE_CHECK OR UPDATE_CHECK = " " OR UPDATE_CHECK = "true" OR UPDATE_CHECK = "false" OR UPDATE_CHECK = "True" OR UPDATE_CHECK = "False" OR UPDATE_CHECK = "TRUE" OR UPDATE_CHECK = "FALSE" OR UPDATE_CHECK = "t" OR UPDATE_CHECK = "f" OR UPDATE_CHECK = "T" OR UPDATE_CHECK = "F" OR UPDATE_CHECK = "1" OR UPDATE_CHECK = "0"'
            Message='UPDATE_CHECK must be true or false. To give it, run the installer from a command prompt run as administrator: msiexec /i "[OriginalDatabase]" [UpdateCheckFix]' />

    <!--
      Derived from the options, once all of them are known. Without WEBSITE=1
      the service only serves the usage to a hub, and ignores HUB_DEVICES and
      HUB_ROUTERS. DataOnly, ServiceHubDevices and ServiceHubRouters are private (mixed case), so the setup
      wizard does not pass them on to the install: they are worked out there
      from WEBSITE as the wizard left it, also when the wizard switched it
      from the remembered 1 to 0.
    -->
    <SetProperty Action="DefaultPORT" Id="PORT" Value="9393" After="LaunchConditions" Sequence="both" Condition='NOT PORT OR PORT = " "' />
    <SetProperty Action="DefaultWEBSITE" Id="WEBSITE" Value="0" After="LaunchConditions" Sequence="both" Condition="NOT WEBSITE" />
    <Property Id="DataOnly" Value="true" />
    <SetProperty Action="WebsiteOn" Id="DataOnly" Value="false" After="DefaultWEBSITE" Sequence="execute" Condition='WEBSITE = "1"' />
    <SetProperty Action="WebsiteHubDevices" Id="ServiceHubDevices" Value="[HUB_DEVICES]" After="DefaultWEBSITE" Sequence="execute" Condition='WEBSITE = "1"' />
    <SetProperty Action="WebsiteHubRouters" Id="ServiceHubRouters" Value="[HUB_ROUTERS]" After="DefaultWEBSITE" Sequence="execute" Condition='WEBSITE = "1"' />

    <!--
      The router passwords are not remembered with the other options: the
      installer writes them to a registry key that only the system,
      administrators and the service's account can read (RouterPasswords),
      and the service moves them from there into a file in its data folder,
      encrypted with DPAPI, and deletes them from the registry. Hidden keeps
      them out of the installer's log. ROUTER_SECRET is the password field on
      the Router page (see RouterDlg).
    -->
    <Property Id="ROUTER_PASSWORDS" Secure="yes" Hidden="yes" />
    <Property Id="ROUTER_SECRET" Hidden="yes" />

    <!--
      Each add-on's box on the Add-ons page has a property of its own, ticked
      when the add-on is on. A cleared box leaves its property empty, which
      would let AppSearch bring back the remembered 1, so Next turns the box
      into 1 or 0 for the add-on's option (see AddOnsDlg).

      Any other value than 1 counts as off, and as an update or repair checks
      the add-on again (see the power add-on), a typo such as POWER=true would
      remove it. So, like UPDATE_CHECK, each add-on option takes only 0 or 1,
      also a remembered one, and the refusal names the command that gives it
      ($(AddOn)Fix, a repair for the installed version). An uninstall is not
      held up by it.
    -->
    <?foreach AddOn in POWER;GPU;PRESSURE;KERNEL;WIFI;MEMORY;PORTS;SMART;PROCESSES ?>
    <SetProperty Action="Box$(AddOn)" Id="$(AddOn)_BOX" Value="1" After="LaunchConditions" Sequence="ui" Condition='$(AddOn) = "1"' />
    <Property Id="$(AddOn)Fix" Value="$(AddOn)=1" />
    <SetProperty Action="$(AddOn)FixInstalled" Id="$(AddOn)Fix" Value="REINSTALL=ALL REINSTALLMODE=m $(AddOn)=1" Before="LaunchConditions" Sequence="both" Condition="Installed" />
    <Launch Condition='REMOVE = "ALL" OR NOT $(AddOn) OR $(AddOn) = "0" OR $(AddOn) = "1"'
            Message='$(AddOn) must be 0 or 1. To give it, run the installer from a command prompt run as administrator: msiexec /i "[OriginalDatabase]" [$(AddOn)Fix], or with $(AddOn)=0 to remove the add-on' />
    <!-- The remembered value once more, kept as it is, to tell whether a repair changes the add-on (AddOnFiles). -->
    <Property Id="REMEMBERED_$(AddOn)">
      <RegistrySearch Id="RememberedAgain$(AddOn)" Root="HKLM" Key="SOFTWARE\Usage Control" Name="$(AddOn)" Type="raw" />
    </Property>
    <?endforeach?>

    <!--
      A repair with REINSTALLMODE=m copies and deletes no file, also not the
      program of an add-on it adds or removes. So a repair that changes an
      add-on option from the remembered one, with no letter in REINSTALLMODE
      for the files, also reinstalls missing or older files (o); the other
      files are the same, so it copies none of them. It sets this before
      costing, which reads it. It compares the values rather than asking
      whether the option was given, as with a user interface the remembered
      options reach the install as if given; a repair that gives the
      remembered value, or one without the option, keeps REINSTALLMODE=m and
      so the tray icons. Where nothing is remembered, as after an install
      before the add-on existed, an option given as 1 or 0 counts as a change.
    -->
    <SetProperty Action="AddOnFiles" Id="REINSTALLMODE" Value="omus" Before="CostInitialize" Sequence="execute"
                 Condition='REINSTALL AND NOT (REINSTALLMODE ~&gt;&lt; "p" OR REINSTALLMODE ~&gt;&lt; "o" OR REINSTALLMODE ~&gt;&lt; "e" OR REINSTALLMODE ~&gt;&lt; "d" OR REINSTALLMODE ~&gt;&lt; "c" OR REINSTALLMODE ~&gt;&lt; "a") AND (POWER &lt;&gt; REMEMBERED_POWER OR GPU &lt;&gt; REMEMBERED_GPU OR PRESSURE &lt;&gt; REMEMBERED_PRESSURE OR KERNEL &lt;&gt; REMEMBERED_KERNEL OR WIFI &lt;&gt; REMEMBERED_WIFI OR MEMORY &lt;&gt; REMEMBERED_MEMORY OR PORTS &lt;&gt; REMEMBERED_PORTS OR SMART &lt;&gt; REMEMBERED_SMART OR PROCESSES &lt;&gt; REMEMBERED_PROCESSES)' />

    <!--
      The edition on the Edition page: Cub installs the lightweight add-ons
      (kernel, memory, pressure, Wi-Fi), Grizzly those and the add-ons picked
      on the Add-ons page. Not remembered itself: an update starts at Grizzly
      when one of its add-ons is installed.
    -->
    <Property Id="EDITION" Value="cub" />
    <SetProperty Action="EditionGrizzly" Id="EDITION" Value="grizzly" After="LaunchConditions" Sequence="ui"
                 Condition='POWER = "1" OR GPU = "1" OR PROCESSES = "1" OR PORTS = "1" OR SMART = "1"' />

    <!--
      The router on the Router page, shown with the website on (hub mode):
      ROUTER_KIND is the way the hub reads it, and Next turns the page into
      HUB_ROUTERS and ROUTER_PASSWORDS. An update starts at keeping the
      routers it has.
    -->
    <Property Id="ROUTER_KIND" Value="none" />
    <Property Id="ROUTER_NAME" Value="Router" />
    <SetProperty Action="RouterKeep" Id="ROUTER_KIND" Value="keep" After="LaunchConditions" Sequence="ui"
                 Condition='HUB_ROUTERS AND HUB_ROUTERS &lt;&gt; " "' />

    <!-- The setup wizard: welcome, the Mode page, the Router page with the website on, the Edition page, the Add-ons page for Grizzly, ready to install. -->
    <UIRef Id="SetupWizard" />
    <!-- No "Change" in Apps and Features: a newer installer, or a repair with WEBSITE, changes the mode. -->
    <Property Id="ARPNOMODIFY" Value="1" />

    <StandardDirectory Id="ProgramFiles64Folder">
      <Directory Id="INSTALLFOLDER" Name="Usage Control" />
    </StandardDirectory>
    <StandardDirectory Id="CommonAppDataFolder">
      <Directory Id="DATAFOLDER" Name="Usage Control">
        <!-- Where add-ons write what they read, for the service to show. -->
        <Directory Id="ADDONSFOLDER" Name="addons" />
      </Directory>
    </StandardDirectory>

    <Component Directory="INSTALLFOLDER">
      <File Source="$(Binary)" Name="usage-control.exe" KeyPath="yes" />

      <!-- A low-privilege account: reading the usage needs no administrator rights. -->
      <ServiceInstall Name="UsageControl"
                      DisplayName="Usage Control"
                      Description="Serves the usage of this computer on the local network."
                      Type="ownProcess"
                      Start="auto"
                      ErrorControl="normal"
                      Account="NT AUTHORITY\LocalService">
        <!--
          Windows' default rights for services, except that logged-in users
          (IU) may also start (RP) and stop (WP) this one, from the tray icon.
        -->
        <PermissionEx Sddl="D:(A;;CCLCSWRPWPDTLOCRRC;;;SY)(A;;CCDCLCSWRPWPDTLOCRSDRCWDWO;;;BA)(A;;CCLCSWRPWPLOCRRC;;;IU)(A;;CCLCSWLOCRRC;;;SU)" />
        <util:ServiceConfig FirstFailureActionType="restart"
                            SecondFailureActionType="restart"
                            ThirdFailureActionType="restart"
                            RestartServiceDelayInSeconds="10"
                            ResetPeriodInDays="1" />
      </ServiceInstall>
      <ServiceControl Name="UsageControl" Start="install" Stop="both" Remove="uninstall" Wait="yes" />

      <!-- The service's settings, read by the program as environment variables. -->
      <RegistryValue Root="HKLM" Key="SYSTEM\CurrentControlSet\Services\UsageControl" Name="Environment" Type="multiString">
        <MultiStringValue Value="LISTEN_ADDR=:[PORT]" />
        <MultiStringValue Value="DATA_ONLY=[DataOnly]" />
        <MultiStringValue Value="DATABASE_PATH=[DATAFOLDER]usage-control.db" />
        <MultiStringValue Value="DEVICE_NAME=[DEVICE_NAME]" />
        <MultiStringValue Value="HUB_DEVICES=[ServiceHubDevices]" />
        <MultiStringValue Value="HUB_ROUTERS=[ServiceHubRouters]" />
        <MultiStringValue Value="RETENTION_DAYS=[RETENTION_DAYS]" />
        <MultiStringValue Value="HISTORY_MAX_ENTRIES=[HISTORY_MAX_ENTRIES]" />
        <MultiStringValue Value="BUFFER_HOURS=[BUFFER_HOURS]" />
        <MultiStringValue Value="ALLOWED_HOSTS=[ALLOWED_HOSTS]" />
        <MultiStringValue Value="DISK_PATHS=[DISK_PATHS]" />
        <MultiStringValue Value="UPDATE_CHECK=[UPDATE_CHECK]" />
        <MultiStringValue Value="RESET_PASSWORD=[ServiceResetPassword]" />
        <MultiStringValue Value="ADDONS_DIR=[ADDONSFOLDER]" />
      </RegistryValue>

      <!-- The options, read back by the next update, and the bugfix number (see Upgrade). -->
      <RegistryValue Root="HKLM" Key="SOFTWARE\Usage Control" Name="Bugfix" Type="string" Value="$(Bugfix)" />
      <?foreach Setting in PORT;WEBSITE;DEVICE_NAME;HUB_DEVICES;HUB_ROUTERS;RETENTION_DAYS;HISTORY_MAX_ENTRIES;BUFFER_HOURS;ALLOWED_HOSTS;DISK_PATHS;UPDATE_CHECK;POWER;GPU;PRESSURE;KERNEL;WIFI;MEMORY;PORTS;SMART;PROCESSES ?>
      <RegistryValue Root="HKLM" Key="SOFTWARE\Usage Control" Name="$(Setting)" Type="string" Value="[$(Setting)]" />
      <?endforeach?>

      <!-- Lets errors the service writes to the Application event log show as plain text. -->
      <RegistryValue Root="HKLM" Key="SYSTEM\CurrentControlSet\Services\EventLog\Application\UsageControl"
                     Name="EventMessageFile" Type="expandable" Value="%SystemRoot%\System32\EventCreate.exe" />
      <RegistryValue Root="HKLM" Key="SYSTEM\CurrentControlSet\Services\EventLog\Application\UsageControl"
                     Name="TypesSupported" Type="integer" Value="7" />

      <!-- The program itself also refuses requests from outside the local network. -->
      <fw:FirewallException Name="Usage Control" Port="[PORT]" Protocol="tcp" Profile="private" />
    </Component>

    <!--
      The router passwords given to this install, for the service to encrypt
      (see ROUTER_PASSWORDS). The key is readable only by the system,
      administrators and the service's account, which may also delete the
      value; it is locked down as it is created. Transitive puts the
      passwords there again in an update or repair that is given new ones.
    -->
    <Component Id="RouterPasswords" Directory="INSTALLFOLDER" Guid="5B0E7D51-3C55-4E0B-9D7A-6E1A9C2F4B83"
               Condition='ROUTER_PASSWORDS AND WEBSITE = "1"' Transitive="yes">
      <RegistryKey Root="HKLM" Key="SOFTWARE\Usage Control\Router">
        <PermissionEx Sddl="D:P(A;;KA;;;SY)(A;;KA;;;BA)(A;;0x2001b;;;LS)" />
        <RegistryValue Name="Passwords" Type="string" Value="[ROUTER_PASSWORDS]" KeyPath="yes" />
      </RegistryKey>
    </Component>

    <!-- The tray icon, started for everyone who logs in. -->
    <Component Directory="INSTALLFOLDER">
      <File Id="TrayExe" Source="$(TrayBinary)" Name="usage-control-tray.exe" KeyPath="yes" />
      <RegistryValue Root="HKLM" Key="SOFTWARE\Microsoft\Windows\CurrentVersion\Run" Name="Usage Control"
                     Type="string" Value="&quot;[#TrayExe]&quot;" />
    </Component>
    <!--
      Updating and uninstalling close the tray icons that run, so the file can
      be replaced. A repair that only rewrites the registry (REINSTALLMODE=m,
      as for RESET_PASSWORD) replaces no file, so it leaves them running;
      one that changes an add-on checks the files too (AddOnFiles), so it
      closes them.
    -->
    <util:CloseApplication Target="usage-control-tray.exe" CloseMessage="yes" ElevatedCloseMessage="yes" RebootPrompt="no"
                           Condition='NOT (REINSTALL AND REINSTALLMODE = "m")' />

    <!--
      The power add-on, with POWER=1: a service of its own, in the same
      low-privilege account, that writes to the add-on folder. An update
      checks the condition again, so it also adds or removes the add-on.
      Each add-on is transitive, so a repair checks it again too (REINSTALL=ALL
      REINSTALLMODE=m POWER=1 adds it, POWER=0 removes it). A repair without
      the option reads the remembered one, which the service's component
      writes back, so it keeps the add-ons as they are. A repair that only
      rewrites the registry (REINSTALLMODE=m) would add or remove the
      add-on's service but copy or delete no file, so a repair that changes
      an add-on also checks the files (AddOnFiles).
    -->
    <Component Directory="INSTALLFOLDER" Condition='POWER = "1"' Transitive="yes">
      <File Source="$(AddOns)\usage-control-power.exe" Name="usage-control-power.exe" KeyPath="yes" />
      <ServiceInstall Name="UsageControlPower"
                      DisplayName="Usage Control power add-on"
                      Description="Reads the power this computer draws for Usage Control."
                      Type="ownProcess"
                      Start="auto"
                      ErrorControl="normal"
                      Account="NT AUTHORITY\LocalService">
        <util:ServiceConfig FirstFailureActionType="restart"
                            SecondFailureActionType="restart"
                            ThirdFailureActionType="restart"
                            RestartServiceDelayInSeconds="10"
                            ResetPeriodInDays="1" />
      </ServiceInstall>
      <ServiceControl Name="UsageControlPower" Start="install" Stop="both" Remove="uninstall" Wait="yes" />
      <RegistryValue Root="HKLM" Key="SYSTEM\CurrentControlSet\Services\UsageControlPower" Name="Environment" Type="multiString">
        <MultiStringValue Value="ADDONS_DIR=[ADDONSFOLDER]" />
      </RegistryValue>
      <RegistryValue Root="HKLM" Key="SYSTEM\CurrentControlSet\Services\EventLog\Application\UsageControlPower"
                     Name="EventMessageFile" Type="expandable" Value="%SystemRoot%\System32\EventCreate.exe" />
      <RegistryValue Root="HKLM" Key="SYSTEM\CurrentControlSet\Services\EventLog\Application\UsageControlPower"
                     Name="TypesSupported" Type="integer" Value="7" />
    </Component>

    <!--
      The gpu add-on, with GPU=1, like the power add-on.
    -->
    <Component Directory="INSTALLFOLDER" Condition='GPU = "1"' Transitive="yes">
      <File Source="$(AddOns)\usage-control-gpu.exe" Name="usage-control-gpu.exe" KeyPath="yes" />
      <ServiceInstall Name="UsageControlGPU"
                      DisplayName="Usage Control gpu add-on"
                      Description="Reads the fan, clocks and more of the NVIDIA graphics card for Usage Control."
                      Type="ownProcess"
                      Start="auto"
                      ErrorControl="normal"
                      Account="NT AUTHORITY\LocalService">
        <util:ServiceConfig FirstFailureActionType="restart"
                            SecondFailureActionType="restart"
                            ThirdFailureActionType="restart"
                            RestartServiceDelayInSeconds="10"
                            ResetPeriodInDays="1" />
      </ServiceInstall>
      <ServiceControl Name="UsageControlGPU" Start="install" Stop="both" Remove="uninstall" Wait="yes" />
      <RegistryValue Root="HKLM" Key="SYSTEM\CurrentControlSet\Services\UsageControlGPU" Name="Environment" Type="multiString">
        <MultiStringValue Value="ADDONS_DIR=[ADDONSFOLDER]" />
      </RegistryValue>
      <RegistryValue Root="HKLM" Key="SYSTEM\CurrentControlSet\Services\EventLog\Application\UsageControlGPU"
                     Name="EventMessageFile" Type="expandable" Value="%SystemRoot%\System32\EventCreate.exe" />
      <RegistryValue Root="HKLM" Key="SYSTEM\CurrentControlSet\Services\EventLog\Application\UsageControlGPU"
                     Name="TypesSupported" Type="integer" Value="7" />
    </Component>

    <!--
      The pressure add-on, with PRESSURE=1, like the power add-on. Any account
      may read the performance counters it reads.
    -->
    <Component Directory="INSTALLFOLDER" Condition='PRESSURE = "1"' Transitive="yes">
      <File Source="$(AddOns)\usage-control-pressure.exe" Name="usage-control-pressure.exe" KeyPath="yes" />
      <ServiceInstall Name="UsageControlPressure"
                      DisplayName="Usage Control pressure add-on"
                      Description="Reads how much waits for the processor, memory and disks for Usage Control."
                      Type="ownProcess"
                      Start="auto"
                      ErrorControl="normal"
                      Account="NT AUTHORITY\LocalService">
        <util:ServiceConfig FirstFailureActionType="restart"
                            SecondFailureActionType="restart"
                            ThirdFailureActionType="restart"
                            RestartServiceDelayInSeconds="10"
                            ResetPeriodInDays="1" />
      </ServiceInstall>
      <ServiceControl Name="UsageControlPressure" Start="install" Stop="both" Remove="uninstall" Wait="yes" />
      <RegistryValue Root="HKLM" Key="SYSTEM\CurrentControlSet\Services\UsageControlPressure" Name="Environment" Type="multiString">
        <MultiStringValue Value="ADDONS_DIR=[ADDONSFOLDER]" />
      </RegistryValue>
      <RegistryValue Root="HKLM" Key="SYSTEM\CurrentControlSet\Services\EventLog\Application\UsageControlPressure"
                     Name="EventMessageFile" Type="expandable" Value="%SystemRoot%\System32\EventCreate.exe" />
      <RegistryValue Root="HKLM" Key="SYSTEM\CurrentControlSet\Services\EventLog\Application\UsageControlPressure"
                     Name="TypesSupported" Type="integer" Value="7" />
    </Component>

    <!--
      The kernel add-on, with KERNEL=1, like the power add-on. Its
      performance counters, GetPerformanceInfo and GetTcpStatisticsEx need
      no more than the Local Service account.
    -->
    <Component Directory="INSTALLFOLDER" Condition='KERNEL = "1"' Transitive="yes">
      <File Source="$(AddOns)\usage-control-kernel.exe" Name="usage-control-kernel.exe" KeyPath="yes" />
      <ServiceInstall Name="UsageControlKernel"
                      DisplayName="Usage Control kernel add-on"
                      Description="Reads what Windows is busy with for Usage Control."
                      Type="ownProcess"
                      Start="auto"
                      ErrorControl="normal"
                      Account="NT AUTHORITY\LocalService">
        <util:ServiceConfig FirstFailureActionType="restart"
                            SecondFailureActionType="restart"
                            ThirdFailureActionType="restart"
                            RestartServiceDelayInSeconds="10"
                            ResetPeriodInDays="1" />
      </ServiceInstall>
      <ServiceControl Name="UsageControlKernel" Start="install" Stop="both" Remove="uninstall" Wait="yes" />
      <RegistryValue Root="HKLM" Key="SYSTEM\CurrentControlSet\Services\UsageControlKernel" Name="Environment" Type="multiString">
        <MultiStringValue Value="ADDONS_DIR=[ADDONSFOLDER]" />
      </RegistryValue>
      <RegistryValue Root="HKLM" Key="SYSTEM\CurrentControlSet\Services\EventLog\Application\UsageControlKernel"
                     Name="EventMessageFile" Type="expandable" Value="%SystemRoot%\System32\EventCreate.exe" />
      <RegistryValue Root="HKLM" Key="SYSTEM\CurrentControlSet\Services\EventLog\Application\UsageControlKernel"
                     Name="TypesSupported" Type="integer" Value="7" />
    </Component>

    <!--
      The Wi-Fi add-on, with WIFI=1, in the same low-privilege account: the
      Native Wifi API lets any account read the signal of the connections.
    -->
    <Component Directory="INSTALLFOLDER" Condition='WIFI = "1"' Transitive="yes">
      <File Source="$(AddOns)\usage-control-wifi.exe" Name="usage-control-wifi.exe" KeyPath="yes" />
      <ServiceInstall Name="UsageControlWifi"
                      DisplayName="Usage Control Wi-Fi add-on"
                      Description="Reads the Wi-Fi signal of this computer for Usage Control."
                      Type="ownProcess"
                      Start="auto"
                      ErrorControl="normal"
                      Account="NT AUTHORITY\LocalService">
        <util:ServiceConfig FirstFailureActionType="restart"
                            SecondFailureActionType="restart"
                            ThirdFailureActionType="restart"
                            RestartServiceDelayInSeconds="10"
                            ResetPeriodInDays="1" />
      </ServiceInstall>
      <ServiceControl Name="UsageControlWifi" Start="install" Stop="both" Remove="uninstall" Wait="yes" />
      <RegistryValue Root="HKLM" Key="SYSTEM\CurrentControlSet\Services\UsageControlWifi" Name="Environment" Type="multiString">
        <MultiStringValue Value="ADDONS_DIR=[ADDONSFOLDER]" />
      </RegistryValue>
      <RegistryValue Root="HKLM" Key="SYSTEM\CurrentControlSet\Services\EventLog\Application\UsageControlWifi"
                     Name="EventMessageFile" Type="expandable" Value="%SystemRoot%\System32\EventCreate.exe" />
      <RegistryValue Root="HKLM" Key="SYSTEM\CurrentControlSet\Services\EventLog\Application\UsageControlWifi"
                     Name="TypesSupported" Type="integer" Value="7" />
    </Component>

    <!-- The memory add-on, with MEMORY=1, set up like the power add-on. -->
    <Component Directory="INSTALLFOLDER" Condition='MEMORY = "1"' Transitive="yes">
      <File Source="$(AddOns)\usage-control-memory.exe" Name="usage-control-memory.exe" KeyPath="yes" />
      <ServiceInstall Name="UsageControlMemory"
                      DisplayName="Usage Control memory add-on"
                      Description="Reads details of this computer's memory for Usage Control."
                      Type="ownProcess"
                      Start="auto"
                      ErrorControl="normal"
                      Account="NT AUTHORITY\LocalService">
        <util:ServiceConfig FirstFailureActionType="restart"
                            SecondFailureActionType="restart"
                            ThirdFailureActionType="restart"
                            RestartServiceDelayInSeconds="10"
                            ResetPeriodInDays="1" />
      </ServiceInstall>
      <ServiceControl Name="UsageControlMemory" Start="install" Stop="both" Remove="uninstall" Wait="yes" />
      <RegistryValue Root="HKLM" Key="SYSTEM\CurrentControlSet\Services\UsageControlMemory" Name="Environment" Type="multiString">
        <MultiStringValue Value="ADDONS_DIR=[ADDONSFOLDER]" />
      </RegistryValue>
      <RegistryValue Root="HKLM" Key="SYSTEM\CurrentControlSet\Services\EventLog\Application\UsageControlMemory"
                     Name="EventMessageFile" Type="expandable" Value="%SystemRoot%\System32\EventCreate.exe" />
      <RegistryValue Root="HKLM" Key="SYSTEM\CurrentControlSet\Services\EventLog\Application\UsageControlMemory"
                     Name="TypesSupported" Type="integer" Value="7" />
    </Component>

    <!-- The ports add-on, with PORTS=1, like the power add-on. -->
    <Component Directory="INSTALLFOLDER" Condition='PORTS = "1"' Transitive="yes">
      <File Source="$(AddOns)\usage-control-ports.exe" Name="usage-control-ports.exe" KeyPath="yes" />
      <ServiceInstall Name="UsageControlPorts"
                      DisplayName="Usage Control ports add-on"
                      Description="Lists the ports this computer listens on for Usage Control."
                      Type="ownProcess"
                      Start="auto"
                      ErrorControl="normal"
                      Account="NT AUTHORITY\LocalService">
        <util:ServiceConfig FirstFailureActionType="restart"
                            SecondFailureActionType="restart"
                            ThirdFailureActionType="restart"
                            RestartServiceDelayInSeconds="10"
                            ResetPeriodInDays="1" />
      </ServiceInstall>
      <ServiceControl Name="UsageControlPorts" Start="install" Stop="both" Remove="uninstall" Wait="yes" />
      <RegistryValue Root="HKLM" Key="SYSTEM\CurrentControlSet\Services\UsageControlPorts" Name="Environment" Type="multiString">
        <MultiStringValue Value="ADDONS_DIR=[ADDONSFOLDER]" />
      </RegistryValue>
      <RegistryValue Root="HKLM" Key="SYSTEM\CurrentControlSet\Services\EventLog\Application\UsageControlPorts"
                     Name="EventMessageFile" Type="expandable" Value="%SystemRoot%\System32\EventCreate.exe" />
      <RegistryValue Root="HKLM" Key="SYSTEM\CurrentControlSet\Services\EventLog\Application\UsageControlPorts"
                     Name="TypesSupported" Type="integer" Value="7" />
    </Component>

    <!--
      The smart add-on, with SMART=1: reads the disks' health with the SMART
      commands of the disk drivers (SATA) and the NVMe driver's health log.
      Windows lets only administrators and LocalSystem open a disk for the
      read and write access these commands need, so this service runs as
      LocalSystem, unlike the others. It sends only commands that read.
      Before reading a disk it asks Windows whether it has switched the disk
      off, and a SATA disk also whether it sleeps, and leaves it asleep.
    -->
    <Component Directory="INSTALLFOLDER" Condition='SMART = "1"' Transitive="yes">
      <File Source="$(AddOns)\usage-control-smart.exe" Name="usage-control-smart.exe" KeyPath="yes" />
      <ServiceInstall Name="UsageControlSmart"
                      DisplayName="Usage Control smart add-on"
                      Description="Reads the health of this computer's disks for Usage Control."
                      Type="ownProcess"
                      Start="auto"
                      ErrorControl="normal"
                      Account="LocalSystem">
        <util:ServiceConfig FirstFailureActionType="restart"
                            SecondFailureActionType="restart"
                            ThirdFailureActionType="restart"
                            RestartServiceDelayInSeconds="10"
                            ResetPeriodInDays="1" />
      </ServiceInstall>
      <ServiceControl Name="UsageControlSmart" Start="install" Stop="both" Remove="uninstall" Wait="yes" />
      <RegistryValue Root="HKLM" Key="SYSTEM\CurrentControlSet\Services\UsageControlSmart" Name="Environment" Type="multiString">
        <MultiStringValue Value="ADDONS_DIR=[ADDONSFOLDER]" />
      </RegistryValue>
      <RegistryValue Root="HKLM" Key="SYSTEM\CurrentControlSet\Services\EventLog\Application\UsageControlSmart"
                     Name="EventMessageFile" Type="expandable" Value="%SystemRoot%\System32\EventCreate.exe" />
      <RegistryValue Root="HKLM" Key="SYSTEM\CurrentControlSet\Services\EventLog\Application\UsageControlSmart"
                     Name="TypesSupported" Type="integer" Value="7" />
    </Component>

    <!--
      The processes add-on, with PROCESSES=1, like the power add-on. Local
      Service sees every process: it asks Windows for all of them in one call
      instead of opening each one.
    -->
    <Component Directory="INSTALLFOLDER" Condition='PROCESSES = "1"' Transitive="yes">
      <File Source="$(AddOns)\usage-control-processes.exe" Name="usage-control-processes.exe" KeyPath="yes" />
      <ServiceInstall Name="UsageControlProcesses"
                      DisplayName="Usage Control processes add-on"
                      Description="Lists the processes using the most CPU and memory for Usage Control."
                      Type="ownProcess"
                      Start="auto"
                      ErrorControl="normal"
                      Account="NT AUTHORITY\LocalService">
        <util:ServiceConfig FirstFailureActionType="restart"
                            SecondFailureActionType="restart"
                            ThirdFailureActionType="restart"
                            RestartServiceDelayInSeconds="10"
                            ResetPeriodInDays="1" />
      </ServiceInstall>
      <ServiceControl Name="UsageControlProcesses" Start="install" Stop="both" Remove="uninstall" Wait="yes" />
      <RegistryValue Root="HKLM" Key="SYSTEM\CurrentControlSet\Services\UsageControlProcesses" Name="Environment" Type="multiString">
        <MultiStringValue Value="ADDONS_DIR=[ADDONSFOLDER]" />
      </RegistryValue>
      <RegistryValue Root="HKLM" Key="SYSTEM\CurrentControlSet\Services\EventLog\Application\UsageControlProcesses"
                     Name="EventMessageFile" Type="expandable" Value="%SystemRoot%\System32\EventCreate.exe" />
      <RegistryValue Root="HKLM" Key="SYSTEM\CurrentControlSet\Services\EventLog\Application\UsageControlProcesses"
                     Name="TypesSupported" Type="integer" Value="7" />
    </Component>

    <!--
      The database: the history with WEBSITE=1, otherwise the minutes kept for
      a hub that cannot reach this PC (BUFFER_HOURS). Only the service,
      administrators and the system can read or change it. Uninstalling keeps
      it, so reinstalling keeps the history.
    -->
    <Component Id="DataFolder" Directory="DATAFOLDER" Guid="C3AF9720-FF5B-4D29-AA9B-A3FB303E3586">
      <CreateFolder>
        <PermissionEx Sddl="D:PAI(A;OICI;FA;;;SY)(A;OICI;FA;;;BA)(A;OICI;0x1301bf;;;LS)" />
      </CreateFolder>
    </Component>
    <!-- The add-on folder, with the data folder's rights. -->
    <Component Id="AddOnsFolder" Directory="ADDONSFOLDER" Guid="5E0F3C1B-7D52-4E4B-9C8E-2A6F1D93B4C7">
      <CreateFolder />
    </Component>
  </Package>

  <!--
    The wizard is WiX's minimal one with the Mode page added after the
    welcome page. Silent installs (msiexec /qn) skip it and use WEBSITE.
  -->
  <Fragment>
    <UI Id="SetupWizard">
      <TextStyle Id="WixUI_Font_Normal" FaceName="Tahoma" Size="8" />
      <TextStyle Id="WixUI_Font_Bigger" FaceName="Tahoma" Size="12" />
      <TextStyle Id="WixUI_Font_Title" FaceName="Tahoma" Size="9" Bold="yes" />
      <Property Id="DefaultUIFont" Value="WixUI_Font_Normal" />

      <DialogRef Id="ErrorDlg" />
      <DialogRef Id="FatalError" />
      <DialogRef Id="FilesInUse" />
      <DialogRef Id="MsiRMFilesInUse" />
      <DialogRef Id="PrepareDlg" />
      <DialogRef Id="ProgressDlg" />
      <DialogRef Id="ResumeDlg" />
      <DialogRef Id="UserExit" />

      <Dialog Id="ModeDlg" Width="370" Height="270" Title="[ProductName] Setup">
        <Control Id="BannerBitmap" Type="Bitmap" X="0" Y="0" Width="370" Height="44" TabSkip="no" Text="WixUI_Bmp_Banner" />
        <Control Id="BannerLine" Type="Line" X="0" Y="44" Width="370" Height="0" />
        <Control Id="Title" Type="Text" X="15" Y="6" Width="200" Height="15" Transparent="yes" NoPrefix="yes" Text="{\WixUI_Font_Title}What should this PC do?" />
        <Control Id="Description" Type="Text" X="25" Y="23" Width="280" Height="15" Transparent="yes" NoPrefix="yes" Text="Installing a newer version later can change it." />
        <Control Id="Mode" Type="RadioButtonGroup" X="20" Y="60" Width="330" Height="110" Property="WEBSITE">
          <RadioButtonGroup Property="WEBSITE">
            <RadioButton Value="0" X="0" Y="0" Width="330" Height="15" Text="&amp;Only collect this PC's usage for a hub (recommended)" />
            <RadioButton Value="1" X="0" Y="45" Width="330" Height="15" Text="&amp;Collect and also show the website on this PC" />
          </RadioButtonGroup>
        </Control>
        <Control Id="CollectorText" Type="Text" X="38" Y="77" Width="310" Height="24" NoPrefix="yes" Text="Another device, such as a Raspberry Pi, shows this PC and keeps its history. Nothing else runs here." />
        <Control Id="WebsiteText" Type="Text" X="38" Y="122" Width="310" Height="24" NoPrefix="yes" Text="This PC also shows the page and keeps its own history in C:\ProgramData\Usage Control." />
        <Control Id="BottomLine" Type="Line" X="0" Y="234" Width="370" Height="0" />
        <Control Id="Back" Type="PushButton" X="180" Y="243" Width="56" Height="17" Text="!(loc.WixUIBack)" />
        <Control Id="Next" Type="PushButton" X="236" Y="243" Width="56" Height="17" Default="yes" Text="!(loc.WixUINext)" />
        <Control Id="Cancel" Type="PushButton" X="304" Y="243" Width="56" Height="17" Cancel="yes" Text="!(loc.WixUICancel)">
          <Publish Event="SpawnDialog" Value="CancelDlg" />
        </Control>
      </Dialog>

      <!--
        The router the hub shows as a device, only with the website on. The
        user and password fields show only for the ways that need a login,
        and the community only for SNMP v2c. Next checks the fields for what
        HUB_ROUTERS cannot hold (RouterInvalid) and stays on the page until
        they are fine; the service checks the rest when it starts.
      -->
      <Dialog Id="RouterDlg" Width="370" Height="270" Title="[ProductName] Setup">
        <Control Id="BannerBitmap" Type="Bitmap" X="0" Y="0" Width="370" Height="44" TabSkip="no" Text="WixUI_Bmp_Banner" />
        <Control Id="BannerLine" Type="Line" X="0" Y="44" Width="370" Height="0" />
        <Control Id="Title" Type="Text" X="15" Y="6" Width="200" Height="15" Transparent="yes" NoPrefix="yes" Text="{\WixUI_Font_Title}Should the hub show your router?" />
        <Control Id="Description" Type="Text" X="25" Y="23" Width="300" Height="15" Transparent="yes" NoPrefix="yes" Text="Its traffic and uptime appear as a device of its own." />
        <Control Id="KindLabel" Type="Text" X="20" Y="52" Width="75" Height="12" Text="&amp;Router:" />
        <Control Id="Kind" Type="ComboBox" X="100" Y="50" Width="250" Height="16" Property="ROUTER_KIND" ComboList="yes">
          <ComboBox Property="ROUTER_KIND">
            <ListItem Value="none" Text="No router" />
            <ListItem Value="upnp" Text="Any router with UPnP (no login)" />
            <ListItem Value="fritzbox" Text="FRITZ!Box (user and password)" />
            <ListItem Value="asus" Text="ASUS (user and password)" />
            <ListItem Value="snmp" Text="SNMP v2c (community)" />
            <ListItem Value="snmpv3" Text="SNMPv3 (user and password)" />
            <ListItem Value="keep" Text="Keep the routers set before" />
          </ComboBox>
        </Control>
        <Control Id="KeepText" Type="Text" X="100" Y="74" Width="250" Height="24" ShowCondition='ROUTER_KIND = "keep"' HideCondition='ROUTER_KIND &lt;&gt; "keep"' Text="[HUB_ROUTERS]" />
        <Control Id="AddressLabel" Type="Text" X="20" Y="74" Width="75" Height="12" ShowCondition='(ROUTER_KIND &lt;&gt; "none" AND ROUTER_KIND &lt;&gt; "keep")' HideCondition='NOT (ROUTER_KIND &lt;&gt; "none" AND ROUTER_KIND &lt;&gt; "keep")' Text="&amp;Address:" />
        <Control Id="Address" Type="Edit" X="100" Y="72" Width="250" Height="16" Property="ROUTER_ADDRESS" Text="{253}" ShowCondition='(ROUTER_KIND &lt;&gt; "none" AND ROUTER_KIND &lt;&gt; "keep")' HideCondition='NOT (ROUTER_KIND &lt;&gt; "none" AND ROUTER_KIND &lt;&gt; "keep")' />
        <Control Id="NameLabel" Type="Text" X="20" Y="96" Width="75" Height="12" ShowCondition='(ROUTER_KIND &lt;&gt; "none" AND ROUTER_KIND &lt;&gt; "keep")' HideCondition='NOT (ROUTER_KIND &lt;&gt; "none" AND ROUTER_KIND &lt;&gt; "keep")' Text="&amp;Name on the page:" />
        <Control Id="Name" Type="Edit" X="100" Y="94" Width="250" Height="16" Property="ROUTER_NAME" Text="{64}" ShowCondition='(ROUTER_KIND &lt;&gt; "none" AND ROUTER_KIND &lt;&gt; "keep")' HideCondition='NOT (ROUTER_KIND &lt;&gt; "none" AND ROUTER_KIND &lt;&gt; "keep")' />
        <Control Id="UserLabel" Type="Text" X="20" Y="118" Width="75" Height="12" ShowCondition='(ROUTER_KIND = "asus" OR ROUTER_KIND = "fritzbox" OR ROUTER_KIND = "snmpv3")' HideCondition='NOT (ROUTER_KIND = "asus" OR ROUTER_KIND = "fritzbox" OR ROUTER_KIND = "snmpv3")' Text="&amp;User:" />
        <Control Id="User" Type="Edit" X="100" Y="116" Width="250" Height="16" Property="ROUTER_USER" Text="{64}" ShowCondition='(ROUTER_KIND = "asus" OR ROUTER_KIND = "fritzbox" OR ROUTER_KIND = "snmpv3")' HideCondition='NOT (ROUTER_KIND = "asus" OR ROUTER_KIND = "fritzbox" OR ROUTER_KIND = "snmpv3")' />
        <Control Id="PasswordLabel" Type="Text" X="20" Y="140" Width="75" Height="12" ShowCondition='(ROUTER_KIND = "asus" OR ROUTER_KIND = "fritzbox" OR ROUTER_KIND = "snmpv3")' HideCondition='NOT (ROUTER_KIND = "asus" OR ROUTER_KIND = "fritzbox" OR ROUTER_KIND = "snmpv3")' Text="&amp;Password:" />
        <Control Id="CommunityLabel" Type="Text" X="20" Y="140" Width="75" Height="12" ShowCondition='ROUTER_KIND = "snmp"' HideCondition='ROUTER_KIND &lt;&gt; "snmp"' Text="&amp;Community:" />
        <Control Id="Secret" Type="Edit" X="100" Y="138" Width="250" Height="16" Property="ROUTER_SECRET" Password="yes" Text="{128}" ShowCondition='(ROUTER_KIND = "asus" OR ROUTER_KIND = "fritzbox" OR ROUTER_KIND = "snmpv3" OR ROUTER_KIND = "snmp")' HideCondition='NOT (ROUTER_KIND = "asus" OR ROUTER_KIND = "fritzbox" OR ROUTER_KIND = "snmpv3" OR ROUTER_KIND = "snmp")' />
        <Control Id="PasswordHint" Type="Text" X="100" Y="157" Width="250" Height="20" NoPrefix="yes" ShowCondition='(ROUTER_KIND = "asus" OR ROUTER_KIND = "fritzbox" OR ROUTER_KIND = "snmpv3")' HideCondition='NOT (ROUTER_KIND = "asus" OR ROUTER_KIND = "fritzbox" OR ROUTER_KIND = "snmpv3")' Text="Empty keeps the password stored before for this name. SNMPv3 needs at least 8 characters." />
        <Control Id="CommunityHint" Type="Text" X="100" Y="157" Width="250" Height="20" NoPrefix="yes" ShowCondition='ROUTER_KIND = "snmp"' HideCondition='ROUTER_KIND &lt;&gt; "snmp"' Text="Empty for public, or the community stored before for this name." />
        <Control Id="InvalidText" Type="Text" X="20" Y="179" Width="330" Height="12" NoPrefix="yes" ShowCondition="RouterInvalid" HideCondition="NOT RouterInvalid" Text="Fill in the address, the name and the user, without commas, = or @." />
        <Control Id="PrivacyText" Type="Text" X="20" Y="195" Width="330" Height="36" NoPrefix="yes" Text="Nothing is sent to any server: the hub asks the router on the local network and keeps everything on this PC. The password is encrypted with Windows (DPAPI), so only the service can read it." />
        <Control Id="BottomLine" Type="Line" X="0" Y="234" Width="370" Height="0" />
        <Control Id="Back" Type="PushButton" X="180" Y="243" Width="56" Height="17" Text="!(loc.WixUIBack)" />
        <Control Id="Next" Type="PushButton" X="236" Y="243" Width="56" Height="17" Default="yes" Text="!(loc.WixUINext)" />
        <Control Id="Cancel" Type="PushButton" X="304" Y="243" Width="56" Height="17" Cancel="yes" Text="!(loc.WixUICancel)">
          <Publish Event="SpawnDialog" Value="CancelDlg" />
        </Control>
      </Dialog>

      <Dialog Id="EditionDlg" Width="370" Height="270" Title="[ProductName] Setup">
        <Control Id="BannerBitmap" Type="Bitmap" X="0" Y="0" Width="370" Height="44" TabSkip="no" Text="WixUI_Bmp_Banner" />
        <Control Id="BannerLine" Type="Line" X="0" Y="44" Width="370" Height="0" />
        <Control Id="Title" Type="Text" X="15" Y="6" Width="200" Height="15" Transparent="yes" NoPrefix="yes" Text="{\WixUI_Font_Title}Which edition should it run?" />
        <Control Id="Description" Type="Text" X="25" Y="23" Width="300" Height="15" Transparent="yes" NoPrefix="yes" Text="Each add-on runs as a small service of its own." />
        <Control Id="Edition" Type="RadioButtonGroup" X="20" Y="60" Width="330" Height="110" Property="EDITION">
          <RadioButtonGroup Property="EDITION">
            <RadioButton Value="cub" X="0" Y="0" Width="330" Height="15" Text="&amp;Cub: the lightweight add-ons (recommended)" />
            <RadioButton Value="grizzly" X="0" Y="45" Width="330" Height="15" Text="&amp;Grizzly: Cub and the add-ons you pick next" />
          </RadioButtonGroup>
        </Control>
        <Control Id="CubText" Type="Text" X="38" Y="77" Width="310" Height="24" NoPrefix="yes" Text="Kernel, memory, pressure and Wi-Fi, which read a few counters at each poll." />
        <Control Id="GrizzlyText" Type="Text" X="38" Y="122" Width="310" Height="24" NoPrefix="yes" Text="Adds a choice of power, graphics card, processes, ports and disk health, which cost more or need more rights." />
        <Control Id="BottomLine" Type="Line" X="0" Y="234" Width="370" Height="0" />
        <Control Id="Back" Type="PushButton" X="180" Y="243" Width="56" Height="17" Text="!(loc.WixUIBack)" />
        <Control Id="Next" Type="PushButton" X="236" Y="243" Width="56" Height="17" Default="yes" Text="!(loc.WixUINext)" />
        <Control Id="Cancel" Type="PushButton" X="304" Y="243" Width="56" Height="17" Cancel="yes" Text="!(loc.WixUICancel)">
          <Publish Event="SpawnDialog" Value="CancelDlg" />
        </Control>
      </Dialog>

      <!--
        Grizzly's add-ons to install, one box each with what it tracks in its
        text. Slot i is at Y = 55 + 17 * i, ten slots up to the bottom line.
        Each Grizzly add-on that runs on Windows has a fixed slot, so adding
        one never moves the others; a slot stays empty until its add-on is
        added: 0 power, 1 gpu, 2 processes, 3 ports, 4 smart, 5 to 9 free.
        Cub's add-ons (kernel, memory, pressure, wifi) come with both editions
        and have none, nor have the Linux-only add-ons (inodes, containers).
      -->
      <Dialog Id="AddOnsDlg" Width="370" Height="270" Title="[ProductName] Setup">
        <Control Id="BannerBitmap" Type="Bitmap" X="0" Y="0" Width="370" Height="44" TabSkip="no" Text="WixUI_Bmp_Banner" />
        <Control Id="BannerLine" Type="Line" X="0" Y="44" Width="370" Height="0" />
        <Control Id="Title" Type="Text" X="15" Y="6" Width="200" Height="15" Transparent="yes" NoPrefix="yes" Text="{\WixUI_Font_Title}What else should Grizzly track?" />
        <Control Id="Description" Type="Text" X="25" Y="23" Width="300" Height="15" Transparent="yes" NoPrefix="yes" Text="Cub's lightweight add-ons come with it. These are optional." />
        <Control Id="Power" Type="CheckBox" X="20" Y="55" Width="330" Height="15" Property="POWER_BOX" CheckBoxValue="1" Text="&amp;Power: what the CPU, battery and NVIDIA graphics card draw" />
        <Control Id="Gpu" Type="CheckBox" X="20" Y="72" Width="330" Height="15" Property="GPU_BOX" CheckBoxValue="1" Text="&amp;Graphics card: fan, clocks, video engines and power limit" />
        <Control Id="Processes" Type="CheckBox" X="20" Y="89" Width="330" Height="15" Property="PROCESSES_BOX" CheckBoxValue="1" Text="P&amp;rocesses: the ten using the most CPU and memory" />
        <Control Id="Ports" Type="CheckBox" X="20" Y="106" Width="330" Height="15" Property="PORTS_BOX" CheckBoxValue="1" Text="P&amp;orts: which TCP and UDP ports the PC listens on" />
        <Control Id="Smart" Type="CheckBox" X="20" Y="123" Width="330" Height="15" Property="SMART_BOX" CheckBoxValue="1" Text="&amp;Smart: the disks' health, temperature and wear" />
        <Control Id="BottomLine" Type="Line" X="0" Y="234" Width="370" Height="0" />
        <Control Id="Back" Type="PushButton" X="180" Y="243" Width="56" Height="17" Text="!(loc.WixUIBack)" />
        <Control Id="Next" Type="PushButton" X="236" Y="243" Width="56" Height="17" Default="yes" Text="!(loc.WixUINext)" />
        <Control Id="Cancel" Type="PushButton" X="304" Y="243" Width="56" Height="17" Cancel="yes" Text="!(loc.WixUICancel)">
          <Publish Event="SpawnDialog" Value="CancelDlg" />
        </Control>
      </Dialog>

      <!--
        After an install or update, Finish shows the tray icon when its box is
        ticked. A repair from the wizard closes the icon (see
        CloseApplication), and WiX's last page shows the box only for an
        install, so Finish then shows the icon again without asking.
      -->
      <Publish Dialog="ExitDialog" Control="Finish" Event="DoAction" Value="StartTray" Order="998"
               Condition='(WIXUI_EXITDIALOGOPTIONALCHECKBOX = 1 AND NOT Installed) OR WixUI_InstallMode = "Repair"' />
      <Publish Dialog="ExitDialog" Control="Finish" Event="EndDialog" Value="Return" Order="999" />

      <Publish Dialog="WelcomeDlg" Control="Next" Event="NewDialog" Value="ModeDlg" />
      <Publish Dialog="ModeDlg" Control="Back" Event="NewDialog" Value="WelcomeDlg" />
      <Publish Dialog="ModeDlg" Control="Next" Event="NewDialog" Value="RouterDlg" Condition='WEBSITE = "1"' />
      <Publish Dialog="ModeDlg" Control="Next" Event="NewDialog" Value="EditionDlg" Condition='WEBSITE &lt;&gt; "1"' />
      <Publish Dialog="RouterDlg" Control="Back" Event="NewDialog" Value="ModeDlg" />
      <Publish Dialog="RouterDlg" Control="Next" Property="RouterInvalid" Value="1" Condition='(ROUTER_KIND &lt;&gt; "none" AND ROUTER_KIND &lt;&gt; "keep") AND (NOT ROUTER_ADDRESS OR NOT ROUTER_NAME OR ROUTER_ADDRESS &gt;&lt; "," OR ROUTER_ADDRESS &gt;&lt; "=" OR ROUTER_ADDRESS &gt;&lt; "@" OR ROUTER_ADDRESS &gt;&lt; " " OR ROUTER_NAME &gt;&lt; "," OR ROUTER_NAME &gt;&lt; "=" OR ((ROUTER_KIND = "asus" OR ROUTER_KIND = "fritzbox" OR ROUTER_KIND = "snmpv3") AND (NOT ROUTER_USER OR ROUTER_USER &gt;&lt; "," OR ROUTER_USER &gt;&lt; "=" OR ROUTER_USER &gt;&lt; "@" OR ROUTER_USER &gt;&lt; ":" OR ROUTER_USER &gt;&lt; " ")))' Order="1" />
      <Publish Dialog="RouterDlg" Control="Next" Property="RouterInvalid" Value="{}" Condition='NOT ((ROUTER_KIND &lt;&gt; "none" AND ROUTER_KIND &lt;&gt; "keep") AND (NOT ROUTER_ADDRESS OR NOT ROUTER_NAME OR ROUTER_ADDRESS &gt;&lt; "," OR ROUTER_ADDRESS &gt;&lt; "=" OR ROUTER_ADDRESS &gt;&lt; "@" OR ROUTER_ADDRESS &gt;&lt; " " OR ROUTER_NAME &gt;&lt; "," OR ROUTER_NAME &gt;&lt; "=" OR ((ROUTER_KIND = "asus" OR ROUTER_KIND = "fritzbox" OR ROUTER_KIND = "snmpv3") AND (NOT ROUTER_USER OR ROUTER_USER &gt;&lt; "," OR ROUTER_USER &gt;&lt; "=" OR ROUTER_USER &gt;&lt; "@" OR ROUTER_USER &gt;&lt; ":" OR ROUTER_USER &gt;&lt; " "))))' Order="1" />
      <Publish Dialog="RouterDlg" Control="Next" Property="HUB_ROUTERS" Value=" " Condition='NOT RouterInvalid AND ROUTER_KIND = "none"' Order="2" />
      <Publish Dialog="RouterDlg" Control="Next" Property="HUB_ROUTERS" Value="[ROUTER_NAME]=[ROUTER_KIND]:[ROUTER_ADDRESS]" Condition='NOT RouterInvalid AND (ROUTER_KIND = "upnp" OR ROUTER_KIND = "snmp")' Order="2" />
      <Publish Dialog="RouterDlg" Control="Next" Property="HUB_ROUTERS" Value="[ROUTER_NAME]=[ROUTER_KIND]:[ROUTER_USER]@[ROUTER_ADDRESS]" Condition='NOT RouterInvalid AND (ROUTER_KIND = "asus" OR ROUTER_KIND = "fritzbox" OR ROUTER_KIND = "snmpv3")' Order="2" />
      <Publish Dialog="RouterDlg" Control="Next" Property="ROUTER_PASSWORDS" Value="[ROUTER_NAME]=[ROUTER_SECRET]" Condition='NOT RouterInvalid AND (ROUTER_KIND = "asus" OR ROUTER_KIND = "fritzbox" OR ROUTER_KIND = "snmpv3" OR ROUTER_KIND = "snmp") AND ROUTER_SECRET' Order="2" />
      <Publish Dialog="RouterDlg" Control="Next" Property="ROUTER_PASSWORDS" Value="{}" Condition='NOT RouterInvalid AND ROUTER_KIND &lt;&gt; "keep" AND NOT ((ROUTER_KIND = "asus" OR ROUTER_KIND = "fritzbox" OR ROUTER_KIND = "snmpv3" OR ROUTER_KIND = "snmp") AND ROUTER_SECRET)' Order="2" />
      <Publish Dialog="RouterDlg" Control="Next" Event="NewDialog" Value="EditionDlg" Condition="NOT RouterInvalid" Order="3" />
      <Publish Dialog="EditionDlg" Control="Back" Event="NewDialog" Value="RouterDlg" Condition='WEBSITE = "1"' />
      <Publish Dialog="EditionDlg" Control="Back" Event="NewDialog" Value="ModeDlg" Condition='WEBSITE &lt;&gt; "1"' />
      <!-- Both editions install Cub's add-ons; Cub none of Grizzly's. -->
      <?foreach AddOn in PRESSURE;KERNEL;WIFI;MEMORY ?>
      <Publish Dialog="EditionDlg" Control="Next" Property="$(AddOn)" Value="1" Order="1" />
      <?endforeach?>
      <?foreach AddOn in POWER;GPU;PORTS;SMART;PROCESSES ?>
      <Publish Dialog="EditionDlg" Control="Next" Property="$(AddOn)" Value="0" Condition='EDITION = "cub"' Order="1" />
      <?endforeach?>
      <Publish Dialog="EditionDlg" Control="Next" Event="NewDialog" Value="VerifyReadyDlg" Condition='EDITION = "cub"' Order="2" />
      <Publish Dialog="EditionDlg" Control="Next" Event="NewDialog" Value="AddOnsDlg" Condition='EDITION = "grizzly"' Order="2" />
      <Publish Dialog="AddOnsDlg" Control="Back" Event="NewDialog" Value="EditionDlg" />
      <?foreach AddOn in POWER;GPU;PORTS;SMART;PROCESSES ?>
      <Publish Dialog="AddOnsDlg" Control="Next" Property="$(AddOn)" Value="1" Condition="$(AddOn)_BOX" Order="1" />
      <Publish Dialog="AddOnsDlg" Control="Next" Property="$(AddOn)" Value="0" Condition="NOT $(AddOn)_BOX" Order="1" />
      <?endforeach?>
      <Publish Dialog="AddOnsDlg" Control="Next" Event="NewDialog" Value="VerifyReadyDlg" Order="2" />
      <Publish Dialog="VerifyReadyDlg" Control="Back" Event="NewDialog" Value="AddOnsDlg" Condition='NOT Installed AND EDITION = "grizzly"' />
      <Publish Dialog="VerifyReadyDlg" Control="Back" Event="NewDialog" Value="EditionDlg" Condition='NOT Installed AND EDITION = "cub"' />

      <Publish Dialog="MaintenanceWelcomeDlg" Control="Next" Event="NewDialog" Value="MaintenanceTypeDlg" />
      <Publish Dialog="MaintenanceTypeDlg" Control="RepairButton" Event="NewDialog" Value="VerifyReadyDlg" />
      <Publish Dialog="MaintenanceTypeDlg" Control="RemoveButton" Event="NewDialog" Value="VerifyReadyDlg" />
      <Publish Dialog="MaintenanceTypeDlg" Control="Back" Event="NewDialog" Value="MaintenanceWelcomeDlg" />
      <Publish Dialog="VerifyReadyDlg" Control="Back" Event="NewDialog" Value="MaintenanceTypeDlg" Condition="Installed" />
    </UI>

    <UIRef Id="WixUI_Common" />

    <!-- A ticked box on the wizard's last page shows the tray icon right away. -->
    <SetProperty Id="WIXUI_EXITDIALOGOPTIONALCHECKBOXTEXT" Value="Show the icon in the taskbar now" After="CostFinalize" Sequence="ui" Condition="NOT Installed" />
    <Property Id="WIXUI_EXITDIALOGOPTIONALCHECKBOX" Value="1" />
    <Property Id="WixShellExecTarget" Value="[#TrayExe]" />
    <CustomAction Id="StartTray" BinaryRef="Wix4UtilCA_$(sys.BUILDARCHSHORT)" DllEntry="WixShellExec" Impersonate="yes" Return="ignore" />
  </Fragment>
</Wix>
